```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

class CalculatorTest {
    @Test
    void addsTwoNumbers() {
        Calculator calc = new Calculator();
        assertEquals(5, calc.add(2, 3));
    }
}
```

- Mark each test with `@Test` from `org.junit.jupiter.api`. Methods return `void` and needn't be `public`.
- Assertions are static methods of `Assertions`; the order is **(expected, actual)**.
- Test classes live in `src/test/java`, conventionally named `...Test`, where Maven and Gradle find them.

```java
private ShoppingCart cart;

@BeforeEach
void setUp() {
    cart = new ShoppingCart();
}

@Test
@DisplayName("rejects a negative quantity")
void rejectsNegativeQuantity() {
    IllegalArgumentException ex = assertThrows(
        IllegalArgumentException.class,
        () -> cart.add("apple", -1));
    assertEquals("quantity must be positive", ex.getMessage());
}
```

- `@BeforeEach` runs before **every** test, so each one starts fresh.
- `assertThrows` runs the lambda, fails if it doesn't throw that type, and returns the exception so you can check its message.
- `@DisplayName` sets a readable name for reports.

```java
@ParameterizedTest
@ValueSource(ints = {2, 4, 100})
void evenNumbersAreEven(int n) {
    assertTrue(MathUtils.isEven(n));
}

@ParameterizedTest
@CsvSource({
    "1, 2, 3",
    "0, 0, 0",
    "-5, 5, 0"
})
void adds(int a, int b, int expected) {
    assertEquals(expected, calc.add(a, b));
}
```

One method, many cases — each row runs and is reported as its own test. The annotations live in `junit-jupiter-params`, which the usual `junit-jupiter` dependency already includes.

